    <meta name=3D"description" content=3D"Exhibition. Oct&nbsp;25, 2026=E2=
=80=93Feb&nbsp;27, 2027. =E2=80=9CWhy, it is my studio,=E2=80=9D Constantin=
 Brancusi exclaimed when he first glimpsed New York City=E2=80=99s skyline =
in 1926. =E2=80=9CAll these blocks, all these shapes to be shifted and jugg=
led with, as the experiment grows and changes.=E2=80=9D Brancusi=E2=80=99s =
actual studio, on impasse Ronsin in Paris, was where he lived, worked, ente=
rtained friends and clients, and displayed his sculptures. There, he create=
d some of his most groundbreaking works, which blurred traditional boundari=
es between pedestal and object, movement and stillness, originality and var=
iation. Among them was Bird in Space, notoriously declared by a US customs =
agent to be inadmissible as a work of art. Over four decades, Brancusi=E2=
=80=99s Paris studio evolved from an active space of experimentation to an =
all-encompassing environment=E2=80=94a work of art in itself. Wanting to pr=
eserve the unity of the studio, Brancusi bequeathed its entire contents to =
France in 1957, transforming a home, workshop, and exhibition site into an =
enduring monument. Known today as the Atelier Brancusi, it is a cherished p=
art of Centre Pompidou=E2=80=99s collection in Paris=E2=80=94and, like that=
 museum, it is currently closed for renovations. For the first time in deca=
des, rarely lent sculptures will travel to New York, along with a rich sele=
ction of Brancusi=E2=80=99s photographs, films, hand-carved furniture and p=
edestals, tools, and archival materials. This large-scale exhibition brings=
 these works into dialogue with MoMA=E2=80=99s celebrated Brancusi collecti=
on and several important local loans. Together, they shed new light on Bran=
cusi=E2=80=99s sculptures while revealing his studio as a place of work, pl=
ay, and invention, offering an intimate, complex portrait of an artist who =
redefined sculpture in the 20th century through the radical simplicity of h=
is forms.">
    <meta property=3D"og:description" content=3D"Exhibition. Oct&nbsp;25, 2=
026=E2=80=93Feb&nbsp;27, 2027. =E2=80=9CWhy, it is my studio,=E2=80=9D Cons=
tantin Brancusi exclaimed when he first glimpsed New York City=E2=80=99s sk=
yline in 1926. =E2=80=9CAll these blocks, all these shapes to be shifted an=
d juggled with, as the experiment grows and changes.=E2=80=9D Brancusi=E2=
=80=99s actual studio, on impasse Ronsin in Paris, was where he lived, work=
ed, entertained friends and clients, and displayed his sculptures. There, h=
e created some of his most groundbreaking works, which blurred traditional =
boundaries between pedestal and object, movement and stillness, originality=
 and variation. Among them was Bird in Space, notoriously declared by a US =
customs agent to be inadmissible as a work of art. Over four decades, Branc=
usi=E2=80=99s Paris studio evolved from an active space of experimentation =
to an all-encompassing environment=E2=80=94a work of art in itself. Wanting=
 to preserve the unity of the studio, Brancusi bequeathed its entire conten=
ts to France in 1957, transforming a home, workshop, and exhibition site in=
to an enduring monument. Known today as the Atelier Brancusi, it is a cheri=
shed part of Centre Pompidou=E2=80=99s collection in Paris=E2=80=94and, lik=
e that museum, it is currently closed for renovations. For the first time i=
n decades, rarely lent sculptures will travel to New York, along with a ric=
h selection of Brancusi=E2=80=99s photographs, films, hand-carved furniture=
 and pedestals, tools, and archival materials. This large-scale exhibition =
brings these works into dialogue with MoMA=E2=80=99s celebrated Brancusi co=
llection and several important local loans. Together, they shed new light o=
n Brancusi=E2=80=99s sculptures while revealing his studio as a place of wo=
rk, play, and invention, offering an intimate, complex portrait of an artis=
t who redefined sculpture in the 20th century through the radical simplicit=
y of his forms.">
