  <div class=3D"main-content--restricted" id=3D"description" data-search-ra=
nk=3D"B">
   =20
  <p>=E2=80=9CWhy, it is my studio,=E2=80=9D <a href=3D"https://www.moma.or=
g/artists/738-constantin-brancusi">Constantin Brancusi</a> exclaimed when h=
e first glimpsed New York City=E2=80=99s skyline in 1926. =E2=80=9CAll thes=
e blocks, all these shapes to be shifted and juggled with, as the experimen=
t grows and changes.=E2=80=9D Brancusi=E2=80=99s actual studio, on impasse =
Ronsin in Paris, was where he lived, worked, entertained friends and client=
s, and displayed his sculptures. There, he created some of his most groundb=
reaking works, which blurred traditional boundaries between pedestal and ob=
ject, movement and stillness, originality and variation. Among them was <em=
>Bird in Space</em>, notoriously declared by a US customs agent to be inadm=
issible as a work of art. Over four decades, Brancusi=E2=80=99s Paris studi=
o evolved from an active space of experimentation to an all-encompassing en=
vironment=E2=80=94a work of art in itself.</p>

<p>Wanting to preserve the unity of the studio, Brancusi bequeathed its ent=
ire contents to France in 1957, transforming a home, workshop, and exhibiti=
on site into an enduring monument. Known today as the Atelier Brancusi, it =
is a cherished part of Centre Pompidou=E2=80=99s collection in Paris=E2=80=
=94and, like that museum, it is currently closed for renovations. For the f=
irst time in decades, rarely lent sculptures will travel to New York, along=
 with a rich selection of Brancusi=E2=80=99s photographs, films, hand-carve=
d furniture and pedestals, tools, and archival materials.</p>

<p>This large-scale exhibition brings these works into dialogue with MoMA=
=E2=80=99s celebrated Brancusi collection and several important local loans=
. Together, they shed new light on Brancusi=E2=80=99s sculptures while reve=
aling his studio as a place of work, play, and invention, offering an intim=
ate, complex portrait of an artist who redefined sculpture in the 20th cent=
ury through the radical simplicity of his forms.</p><blockquote id=3D"credi=
ts">
      <p>Organized by Anne Umland, guest curator, and Mia Matthias, Assista=
nt Curator, Department of Painting and Sculpture, with Samantha Small, Cura=
torial Assistant, Department of Painting and Sculpture. We are grateful for=
 the collaboration of Ariane Coulondre, Chief Curator, Department of Modern=
 Collections, and Val=C3=A9rie Loth, Associate Curator, Department of Print=
s and Drawings, Centre Pompidou =E2=80=93 Mus=C3=A9e national d=E2=80=99art=
 moderne, who organized <em>Brancusi: Art Is Just Beginning</em> at Centre =
Pompidou in 2024. This exhibition is presented with the exceptional support=
 of Centre Pompidou, which permanently houses the Atelier Brancusi in Paris=
.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </div>


